From: jfacemyer <1776340+jfacemyer@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ole32: DoDragDrop handles mouse input itself instead of dispatching it.

Wine's drag loop dispatched every non-keyboard message, relying on the
tracker window's capture to route mouse input to the tracker. An
application can take capture back during the drag: Affinity's
FrameworkPopup re-captures the mouse when a child loses capture, which
DoDragDrop's own SetCapture causes. The button release then went to the
popup, Affinity's drag source took it as the end of its own drag and
cleared its state, and dereferenced that state when DoDragDrop returned
-- a NullReferenceException crash reordering Studio tabs.

Windows' drag loop takes mouse input off the queue and tracks it itself;
the drag source sees no mouse messages while DoDragDrop runs, which is
why the same Affinity code does not crash there. Do the same: track
button and move messages in the loop and do not dispatch them. Wheel
messages are still dispatched.

---
diff --git a/dlls/ole32/ole2.c b/dlls/ole32/ole2.c
index c18a46a..d048b35 100644
--- a/dlls/ole32/ole2.c
+++ b/dlls/ole32/ole2.c
@@ -812,10 +812,24 @@ HRESULT WINAPI DoDragDrop (
 	 */
         OLEDD_TrackStateChange(&trackerInfo);
       }
+      else if ((msg.message >= WM_MOUSEMOVE && msg.message <= WM_MBUTTONDBLCLK) ||
+               (msg.message >= WM_XBUTTONDOWN && msg.message <= WM_XBUTTONDBLCLK))
+      {
+        /*
+         * Mouse input is the drag loop's own, as on Windows: track it here
+         * and do not dispatch it. The tracker window holds capture, but an
+         * application can take capture back while the drag is running -- a
+         * popup re-capturing when it sees WM_CAPTURECHANGED does -- and the
+         * button release then reaches that window instead. Applications
+         * that treat a release as the end of their own drag tear their
+         * state down under DoDragDrop, and crash when it returns.
+         */
+        OLEDD_TrackStateChange(&trackerInfo);
+      }
       else
       {
 	/*
-	 * Dispatch the messages only when it's not a keyboard message.
+	 * Dispatch the messages only when it's not a keyboard or mouse message.
 	 */
 	DispatchMessageW(&msg);
       }
